review_comments:
```js
function f() {
    try {
        x();
    } catch (a) {
        var a;
    }
}
```
both `a` resolve to `#3` which is wrong.

You mean the `a` in the catch param and the `a` declared in the catch block is actually different? Although I am still not clear why we should preserve `var a` in this case, I find https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-variablestatements-in-catch-blocks. Does this spec mean they are actually not different?
> They do not shadow the same-named bindings introduced by the [CatchParameter](https://tc39.es/ecma262/#prod-CatchParameter)

I also try:
```js
!function f() {
    var a = 1;
    try {
        throw "error"
    } catch (a) {
        var a = 2;
    }
    console.log(a) // 1
}()
```

> You mean the `a` in the catch param and the `a` declared in the catch block is actually different? Although I am still not clear why we should preserve `var a` in this case, I find https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-variablestatements-in-catch-blocks. Does this spec mean they are actually not different?
> 
> > They do not shadow the same-named bindings introduced by the [CatchParameter](https://tc39.es/ecma262/#prod-CatchParameter)
> 
> I also try:
> 
> ```js
> !function f() {
>     var a = 1;
>     try {
>         throw "error"
>     } catch (a) {
>         var a = 2;
>     }
>     console.log(a) // 1
> }()
> ```

Shadow is what we need. But currently they're considered by SWC as same identifier, which may cause problem.
